  "raw_text": "Step 1: To find \\(997^{-1} \\mod 1000\\), we need to find an integer \\(x\\) such that \\(997x \\equiv 1 \\mod 1000\\).\n\nStep 2: We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find such an \\(x\\). First, apply the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 997 and 1000.\n\nStep 3: Perform the division:\n\\[1000 = 997 \\cdot 1 + 3\\]\n\\[997 = 3 \\cdot 332 + 1\\]\n\\[3 = 3 \\cdot 1 + 0\\]\n\nStep 4: The gcd is 1, which confirms that 997 and 1000 are coprime, and thus an inverse exists.\n\nStep 5: Now, backtrack using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 997 and 1000:\n\\[1 = 997 - 3 \\cdot 332\\]\n\\[3 = 1000 - 997\\]\nSubstitute for 3:\n\\[1 = 997 - (1000 - 997) \\cdot 332\\]\n\\[1 = 997 - 1000 \\cdot 332 + 997 \\cdot 332\\]\n\\[1 = 997 \\cdot 333 - 1000 \\cdot 332\\]\n\nStep 6: From the equation above, we see that \\(997 \\cdot 333 \\equiv 1 \\mod 1000\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{333}<|im_end|>",
